// HumanityOS home design: the HOMESTEAD (55 x 89 m, 1.21 acres), the default start.
// A home design is a body (the same model every ship zone uses: a fixed outer box plus
// freely placed interior walls, openings, lights, structures and room zones) plus the point
// on its shell where a plot's door corridor meets it. It carries NO position on the ship:
// the loader places it at the origin of the player's plot (data/blueprints/ship_structure.ron,
// `plots`), so the same design sits at p1, p2 or any plot of kind "homestead". Everything
// below is in home-local metres from the box's min corner (x east, z south).
// docs/design/ship-homes-and-logistics.md (increment 1a) split this out of the ship file.
//
//   kind  which plots this design fits (a plot's `kind`), and this file's name.
//   door  (x, z) on the shell where the plot's corridor arrives: the east wall at z 40.
//         Every homestead plot's door record must agree with it (the loader checks).
//   body  the home itself. Its `spawn` is where you stand on entering the world.
//
// Materials: 0 = grid, 1 = steel/metal, 2 = concrete, 3 = wood, 4 = glass.
//
// Editor saves rewrite everything BELOW this header from the loaded struct: body comments
// are dropped, floats are re-printed (0.70 -> 0.7), and any field the running build does not
// know is dropped too (a build older than `room_type` strips every room_type on save;
// `just validate-data` then fails loudly, so run it after a save from an older build).
// Durable notes belong here or in docs/design/homestead.md, not beside the records. The home
// file is saved by every play mode; the ship file only by the Dev mode.
//
// Structure pairs (a teleporter's partner): this copy names them by LIST INDEX (`pair: Some(6)`),
// the form an exe built before ship homes increment 4 reads. Such an exe moves a home it cannot
// parse aside as homestead.invalid-<time>.ron, so an older archive launched from this checkout
// must still read it. Builds since increment 4 read either form and write ids
// (`pair: Some("teleporter-2")`) on the next editor Save (src/ship/home_structure.rs
// `settle_structures`).
//
// Zones: `room_type: Some("<data/rooms.ron key>")` on a room-grade zone gives the room the
// zone covers its FUNCTION (purpose, walk-up actions, access); rooms no zone covers stay
// room_N. Since the 2026-09-19 redesign every room in the acre is covered, so a `room_N` id
// appearing again means a wall stopped short and two rooms merged.
//
// THE PLAYER'S ACRE (55 x 89 m, 1.21 acres). Before the redesign 330 m2 of it was
// partitioned and the other 4,565 m2 was one open hall used as a tech-demo floor. It is now
// 23 rooms in four bands along z, with the house in the middle so nothing is a long walk:
//
//   z  0..22   the yard      power terrace (x 0..20) | vehicle bay (x 20..55)
//   z 22..30   the belt      service way (x 0..34)   | orchard court (x 34..55)
//   z 30..51   the middle    plant room (0..12) | forge (12..20) | workshop (20..34) | HOUSE (34..55)
//   z 51..73   the growing   mushroom room + aquaponics (x 0..10) | greenhouse (x 10..55)
//   z 73..89   the far end   fields (x 0..40) | barn (x 40..55)
//
// The house is a 21 x 21 m square entered from the plot's corridor at x=55, z 39..41: an
// entry, a hall across the middle, the quiet rooms north facing the orchard court, and the
// living rooms south behind a glass wall onto the greenhouse. The CONSOLE ROOM (colloquially
// the battlestation; the mothership bridge is the "command deck") is zone "console-room" at
// x 52..55, z 43..51: a narrow den off the entry and the great room, glazed at its south end
// so the garden is behind the screens. `scripts/verify-screens.js` parks there.
//
// Why these numbers: 55 and 89 are consecutive Fibonacci, so the shell is already a golden
// rectangle, and 34/21/22/16 and 13/8/5/3 tile it with no offcuts. The full reasoning, the
// walkthrough, and what each screen is doing where it is: docs/design/player-home.md.

